<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # How Do I Find a Book in the Library? – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> In the Library</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Do I Find a Book in the Library?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Extract main ideas from informational texts.
reading_2: Infer meaning from context clues in short texts.
phonemic_awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can extract main ideas from informational texts about library procedures (e.g., 'Librarians help us find books.').
Can infer meaning from context clues in library-related texts (e.g., 'It's busy in the library, so we speak quietly.').
Can demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words (e.g., recognizing /ɪ/ in 'pick' and /aɪ/ in 'find').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a library or a real book. Students are asked: "What do you see?" and "Where are we?" to elicit previous vocabulary (e.g., library, book, shelf, read). Teacher asks, "What do we do in a library?" to activate prior knowledge about library activities and rules.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models key vocabulary and actions: "We read a book. We find a book. Librarians are helpful." Teacher says single-syllable words like 'pick' and 'find', clearly articulating the short /ɪ/ and long /aɪ/ vowel sounds. The teacher asks students to listen carefully to the vowel sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the library a place to run and shout?" (Expected answer: No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we borrow many books from the library?" (Expected answer: Yes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational text titled "How to Find a Book at the School Library." The text provides simple, sequential instructions using imperatives, modals (should), and target vocabulary (catalog, title, author, section, shelf, non-fiction, fiction, check out, librarian, helpful, quiet). The text is presented on a handout or projector. (Genre: Instructions, Register: Informational, Format: Short text).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Go to the library catalog. You should search for a book by title or author.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Write down the book's section and page number.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Find the correct shelf. Look for your book. There are many books: fiction and non-fiction.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. If you need help, ask a librarian. Librarians are very helpful.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. When you find your book, take it to the checkout desk. You can borrow it for two weeks. Remember to be quiet in the library!</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and interpreting short informational texts related to library procedures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist:</b> Teacher asks, "What is this text about?" (Expected: How to find a book in the library).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements and mark them True or False based on the text.</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">You should shout in the library. (False)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Librarians help us find books. (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">You find a book in the catalog. (False, you search in the catalog, then find it on the shelf).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match words from the text (e.g., 'catalog', 'librarian', 'shelf') with their definitions or actions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Extracting main ideas, inferring meaning from context. Grammar: Imperatives, modals (can/should). Vocabulary: library, catalog, author, title, section, summary, shelf, librarian, check out, borrow, quiet, helpful, fiction, non-fiction. Pronunciation: Phonemic awareness of short and long vowel sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the informational text, but with key vocabulary words (e.g., 'catalog', 'librarian', 'borrow', 'quiet', 'should') missing. They read the sentences and fill in the blanks using a word bank. This reinforces `reading_1` by focusing on sentence-level understanding.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "You ______ look for a book in the _______." (should, catalog)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"A _______ can _______ you find a book." (librarian, help)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled (Inferring Meaning from Context):</b> Students read short sentences or mini-paragraphs that contain unknown words but have context clues. They infer the meaning of the underlined word and choose from multiple-choice options. This addresses `reading_2`.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "The library was very <u>silent</u>, so I could hear the pages turn." (a) loud (b) full (c) quiet (d) busy</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This book is a <u>fiction</u> story about a talking cat." (a) true (b) made-up (c) boring (d) difficult</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Phonemic Awareness Activity:</b> The teacher writes pairs of words from the target vocabulary on the board (e.g., 'pick' / 'find', 'sit' / 'light', 'book' / 'moon' - though 'moon' is not vocabulary, 'book' is). The teacher orally states one word from each pair. Students raise their hand if they hear a 'short' vowel sound or a 'long' vowel sound, practicing identification of /ɪ/ and /aɪ/ in 'pick' and 'find', and similar sounds. The teacher can then ask, "Is the vowel in 'pick' short or long?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs or small groups. They receive 2-3 short, simplified book summaries or descriptions, labeled 'Book A', 'Book B', etc. Each summary includes information about the genre (fiction/non-fiction) and main topic, using target vocabulary (author, title, summary, interesting). Students read each summary aloud in their groups, then discuss and answer guiding questions about each book.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> "Choosing Books to Read." The task requires students to read and process information to make decisions, simulating a real-life library interaction (choosing a book based on its summary).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Each group receives a set of 2-3 "Book Card" handouts. Each card has a 'Title', 'Author', 'Section' (e.g., 'Fiction', 'Non-Fiction: Animals'), and a short 'Summary'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Students read each book summary carefully in their groups.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Groups discuss and answer the following questions for each book on a separate answer sheet, demonstrating `reading_1` (extracting main ideas) and `reading_2` (inferring from context):
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is the main idea of this book?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is this book fiction or non-fiction? How do you know? (inferring from context)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can you infer what the book is generally about from the summary?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If you liked the story, you can _______ it. (borrow/check out)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Teacher monitors, providing support and encouraging use of target vocabulary (e.g., "What section is this book in?", "Who is the author?"). Teacher also encourages polite requests: "Can I borrow this book?" (Pragmatic function).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the collaborative reading and a short individual reading comprehension quiz. The quiz will include a phonemic awareness section.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short, individual reading passage (1-2 paragraphs) about how to take care of library books. This text will contain simple context clues for inference and clear main ideas. After reading, students answer:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What is the main idea of this text? (Reading_1)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Based on the text, what does 'careful' mean when handling books? (Inferring meaning from context, Reading_2)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Is the vowel sound in 'read' short or long? (Phonemic awareness)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Match the word 'return' to its definition: (a) take out (b) give back (c) keep (d) buy.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to accurately extract main ideas from the informational text (reading_1).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to infer meaning of unknown words from context clues (reading_2).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated knowledge of short and long vowel sounds in specified words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of library-related vocabulary during discussions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned about finding books today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reading the instructions to find a book was easy / difficult because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy helped you the most today (e.g., looking for keywords, reading sentence by sentence)?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can knowing the difference between fiction and non-fiction help you in the library?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students successfully extract main ideas from the informational texts? What evidence did I observe?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were students able to infer meaning from context clues effectively? What adjustments are needed for students who struggled?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effective was the phonemic awareness activity for identifying short and long vowel sounds? Should I provide more examples or use different word pairs next time?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in this lesson, and what specific scaffolds might I add for reading challenges in the next lesson?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), we will practice actively speaking and interacting about books, using the library vocabulary we learned. We will ask and answer questions about books we like and practice making polite requests to a librarian."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>